<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stanislaus Hosius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1504">1504</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Stanisław Hozjusz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stanislaus Hosius was a Polish churchman and theologian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Warmia from 1551 and a cardinal from 1561. A leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, he wrote a widely read defence of Catholic doctrine and presided as papal legate over the final sessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>. In 1564 he brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braniewo">Braniewo</a>, founding their first college in the Polish lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stanislaus-hosius.jpg" alt="Stanislaus Hosius" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcello Bacciarelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanis%C5%82aw_Hozjusz_1.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hosius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 5 May 1504, the son of Ulrich Hosse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim">Pforzheim</a>, and spent his youth in Kraków and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. At fifteen, already at home in German, Polish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">University of Kraków</a>, graduating as Bachelor of Arts in 1520. Piotr Tomicki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Kraków and Vice-Chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, made him his secretary and paid for his studies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, where Reginald Pole was a fellow student, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he studied law under Hugo Buoncompagni, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After taking his doctorate in canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> on 8 June 1534, he served in the royal chancery and from 1538 as royal secretary to King Sigismund, who rewarded him with church benefices. He was ordained priest in 1543. Nominated to the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> in 1549, which he accepted reluctantly, he was first sent on a mission to King Ferdinand I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, and was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 23 March 1550. As bishop of Warmia he drew up the Confessio fidei christiana catholica, adopted by the Synod of Piotrków in 1557, and with Marcin Kromer kept Warmia Catholic while neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a> became Protestant.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> sent him as nuncio to Emperor Ferdinand I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to prepare the reopening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> and to win over the emperor's son Maximilian, who appeared to have Protestant sympathies. Made cardinal in 1561, he served as legate with Cardinals Puteo and Gonzaga, mediating between factions and raising Polish questions such as the status of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In December 1563 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> for his diocese to carry out the council's decrees, declining to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> although Cardinal Truchess thought him a candidate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. He became papal legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1566. The lyceum he founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braniewo">Braunsberg</a> became the centre of the Catholic mission among Protestants. He died at Capranica Prenestina, near Rome, on 5 August 1579. A collected edition of his works appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 1584, and his cause for beatification has been opened.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stanislaus Hosius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishop <b>Stanislaus Hosius</b> made it a stronghold of the Counter-Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Capranica, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1552" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stanislaus Hosius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1552">What was here in 1552? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
